{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "id": "chapter-03",
  "bookId": "sleepy-hollow",
  "number": 3,
  "title": "The Morning After",
  "level": "a2",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "p-01",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "The next morning, the village woke to strange "
        },
        {
          "text": "news",
          "vocabId": "news"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Gunpowder, Ichabod’s old horse, came home alone. His saddle was gone, and he was still wearing his reins. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "owner",
          "vocabId": "owner"
        },
        {
          "text": " of the horse looked everywhere, but the teacher did not answer. Soon the whole village began to "
        },
        {
          "text": "search",
          "vocabId": "search"
        },
        {
          "text": " for him. Men took sticks and lamps and walked into the cold woods. The children stayed at home and watched from the windows."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-02",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Near the bridge, they found a path into the woods. A "
        },
        {
          "text": "hat",
          "vocabId": "hat"
        },
        {
          "text": " lay in the stream, and a little further, a "
        },
        {
          "text": "broken ",
          "vocabId": "broken"
        },
        {
          "text": "pumpkin",
          "vocabId": "pumpkin"
        },
        {
          "text": " sat on the ground. There were "
        },
        {
          "text": "footprints",
          "vocabId": "footprints"
        },
        {
          "text": " in the "
        },
        {
          "text": "mud",
          "vocabId": "mud"
        },
        {
          "text": ", but they "
        },
        {
          "text": "disappear",
          "vocabId": "disappear"
        },
        {
          "text": " near the water. The men called Ichabod’s name again and again. Only the echo answered. They looked under the old bridge and behind every tree, but they found no body and no clothes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-03",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "People had many "
        },
        {
          "text": "theories",
          "vocabId": "theories"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Some said the headless rider took the teacher away. Others said Ichabod ran to another town because he was "
        },
        {
          "text": "afraid",
          "vocabId": "afraid"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Brom Bones "
        },
        {
          "text": "laugh",
          "vocabId": "laugh"
        },
        {
          "text": " at every story. He said he knew nothing, but he looked at Katrina and "
        },
        {
          "text": "smile",
          "vocabId": "smile"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Katrina did not cry for long. Soon the village stopped talking about the missing teacher, and the children returned to school."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-04",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "In the "
        },
        {
          "text": "spring",
          "vocabId": "spring"
        },
        {
          "text": ", Katrina married Brom. The village found a new teacher, a quiet young man who did not "
        },
        {
          "text": "believe",
          "vocabId": "believe"
        },
        {
          "text": " in ghosts. But the old farmers still told the "
        },
        {
          "text": "tale",
          "vocabId": "tale"
        },
        {
          "text": " of Ichabod Crane. One of them said he had seen him in a far town, "
        },
        {
          "text": "alive",
          "vocabId": "alive"
        },
        {
          "text": " and happy, with a wife and children. Another said he had become a judge and had forgotten Sleepy Hollow. The old farmers smiled when they said it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-05",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Nobody knew the "
        },
        {
          "text": "truth",
          "vocabId": "truth"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The bridge stayed in its place, and the stream moved under it. On dark nights, when the wind blew through the trees, people still walked home a little faster. And if you listen closely, they say, you can hear a horse on the road — slow, "
        },
        {
          "text": "heavy",
          "vocabId": "heavy"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and never alone. Some stories, the old people say, are stronger than the truth. The valley keeps its secrets. Nobody forgot the name of Ichabod Crane."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
